From: Cardiorentis AG <legal@cardiorentis.example> (Cardiorentis)
To: IQVIA Ltd and IQVIA RDS, Inc. <legal@iqvia.example> (IQVIA)
Date: 2018-03-23
Subject: Complaint filed in the North Carolina Business Court - TRUE-AHF

Cardiorentis has today filed suit against IQVIA Ltd and IQVIA RDS Inc in the North Carolina Business Court.

IQVIA failed to perform its obligations in respect of the TRUE-AHF trial "and then concealed its failures until it was too late to remedy them", with the result that "hundreds of ineligible patients were improperly included in the Clinical Trial, fundamentally undermining the validity of the Clinical Trial and rendering it worthless".

The failure to conduct the trial in accordance with IQVIA's contractual and ethical obligations "was not merely the result of negligence and lack of skill, but ... was the result of an intentional corporate decision to starve the Clinical Trial of resources in order to prop up Quintiles' stock price".

IQVIA "affirmatively misrepresented" in the PD Logs the number of ineligible patients; "it was not until the day that the data was unblinded that Quintiles disclosed to Cardiorentis that a very significant percentage of the patients enrolled in this multi-year Clinical Trial were actually ineligible to participate".

Cardiorentis claims damages, including punitive damages.

--
RECONSTRUCTED FROM THE PUBLISHED JUDGMENT. Not an original document.
Source: Cardiorentis v IQVIA [2022] EWHC 250 (Comm), paras 222, 223.
A court complaint, shown as correspondence because no pre-action letters are described. Cardiorentis relies on these losses as a set-off against the unpaid bills; amount of its claim not stated.
